{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_004","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Structuring content with HTML module","location_hint":"paragraph 94","char_start":1734,"char_end":1770,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_005","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Dynamic scripting with JavaScript module","location_hint":"paragraph 99","char_start":1858,"char_end":1898,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_006","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"WebAssembly is a type of code that can be run in modern web browsers.\nIt is a low-level assembly-like language with a compact binary format that runs with near-native performance and provides languages such as C/C++, C# and Rust with a compilation target so that they can run on the web.","location_hint":"paragraph 137","char_start":2632,"char_end":2919,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_007","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"WebAssembly is designed to complement and run alongside JavaScript — using the WebAssembly JavaScript APIs, you can load WebAssembly modules into a JavaScript app and share functionality between the two. This allows you to take advantage of WebAssembly's performance and power and JavaScript's expressiveness and flexibility in the same app, even if you don't know how to write WebAssembly code.","location_hint":"paragraph 138","char_start":2921,"char_end":3316,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_008","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"WebAssembly has big implications for the web platform, not only because it provides a way for code written in multiple languages to run on the web at near-native speed, but also because it enables client apps to run on the web that previously could not.","location_hint":"paragraph 139","char_start":3318,"char_end":3571,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_009","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"And what's even better is that it is being developed as a web standard via the W3C WebAssembly Working Group and Community Group with active participation from all major browser vendors.","location_hint":"paragraph 140","char_start":3573,"char_end":3759,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_010","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"The WebAssembly guides cover topics such as high-level concepts, compiling from different languages, the textual representation of the Wasm binary format, and how to run WebAssembly.","location_hint":"paragraph 149","char_start":3906,"char_end":4088,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_011","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Get started by reading the high-level concepts behind WebAssembly — what it is, why it is so useful, how it fits into the web platform (and beyond), and how to use it.","location_hint":"paragraph 151","char_start":4114,"char_end":4281,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_012","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Compiling a new C/C++ module to WebAssembly","location_hint":"paragraph 152","char_start":4284,"char_end":4327,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_013","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"When you've written code in C/C++, you can then compile it into Wasm using a tool like Emscripten . Let's look at how it works.","location_hint":"paragraph 153","char_start":4330,"char_end":4457,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_014","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Compiling an existing C module to WebAssembly","location_hint":"paragraph 154","char_start":4460,"char_end":4505,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_015","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"A core use-case for WebAssembly is to take the existing ecosystem of C libraries and allow developers to use them on the web.","location_hint":"paragraph 155","char_start":4508,"char_end":4633,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_016","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Compiling from Rust to WebAssembly","location_hint":"paragraph 156","char_start":4636,"char_end":4670,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_017","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"If you've written some Rust code, you can compile it into WebAssembly! This tutorial takes you through all you need to know to compile a Rust project to Wasm and use it in an existing web app.","location_hint":"paragraph 157","char_start":4673,"char_end":4865,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_018","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Loading and running WebAssembly code","location_hint":"paragraph 158","char_start":4868,"char_end":4904,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_019","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"After you have a Wasm module, this article covers how to fetch, compile and instantiate it, combining the WebAssembly JavaScript API with the Fetch or XHR APIs.","location_hint":"paragraph 159","char_start":4907,"char_end":5067,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_020","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Using the WebAssembly JavaScript API","location_hint":"paragraph 160","char_start":5070,"char_end":5106,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_021","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Once you've loaded a Wasm module, you'll want to use it. In this article, we show you how to use WebAssembly via the WebAssembly JavaScript API.","location_hint":"paragraph 161","char_start":5109,"char_end":5253,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_022","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Exported WebAssembly functions","location_hint":"paragraph 162","char_start":5256,"char_end":5286,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_023","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Exported WebAssembly functions are the JavaScript reflections of WebAssembly functions, which allow calling WebAssembly code from JavaScript. This article describes what they are.","location_hint":"paragraph 163","char_start":5289,"char_end":5468,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_024","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"Understanding WebAssembly text format","location_hint":"paragraph 164","char_start":5471,"char_end":5508,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_0974a54becd4_025","source_id":"src_0974a54becd4","source_hash":"bc8fb56bddc65e82103061d7276ae3eafae3761671dfdece0f878b89d0c9b276","text":"This article explains the Wasm text format. This is the low-level textual representation of a Wasm module shown in browser developer tools when debugging.","location_hint":"paragraph 165","char_start":5511,"char_end":5665,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-10T08:15:01.169Z"}
